---
export const prerender = true
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro"
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro"
import NamePronunciation from "../components/NamePronunciation.astro"
import { allSocials } from "../data/socials"
import { externalRel } from "../utils/linkRel"

const title = "About"
const description =
  "Hi, I'm Gil — curious human, engineer by trade, into music, movement, and thoughtful systems."
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Hero
    title={`<abbr title="northern German for 'Hi'">Moin</abbr>, I'm Gil Desmarais`}
    description="Someone shaped by early internet culture, systems thinking, and a long interest in how technical and human complexity fit together."
  >
    <p>
      Clarity, structure, and curiosity have been the throughline for a long time, in software, in teams, and
      in the way I move through the world.
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <section aria-labelledby="who-i-am" class="width">
    <h2 id="who-i-am" class="accent">Who I am</h2>
    <p>
      Calm, structured, and precise by temperament, I tend to look for the shape of things quickly: where
      patterns connect, and where a small shift in perspective changes the direction entirely. That mix of
      focus, systems thinking, and lateral association shapes how I work, how I communicate, and how I make
      sense of complexity.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="what-shaped-me" class="width">
    <p class="section-kicker">Background</p>
    <h2 id="what-shaped-me" class="accent">What shaped me</h2>
    <p>
      A 28.8k modem, Notepad, and a magazine tutorial about <code>&lt;frames&gt;</code><span class="smiley"
        >:D</span
      > were the entry point. That early internet world left a mark: hands-on, exploratory, and full of small discoveries
      that rewarded patience.
    </p>

    <p>
      Early learning came through making things, breaking them, and figuring out why they worked. JavaScript,
      Perl, PHP, MySQL, Linux, IRC automation, and bootstrapping Gentoo on my only computer were all part of
      that. So was staying connected on the move whenever I could.<sup><a href="#foot-1">1</a></sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      Later, that curiosity crossed into hardware too: soldering circuits, programming microcontrollers, and
      exploring signal processing with Assembler and Delphi. Moving between software and physical systems made
      end-to-end thinking feel natural early on.
    </p>
    <p>
      A period in the German Air Force added structure and responsibility, and studying Business Computer
      Science built a bridge between technical depth and practical context.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="how-i-work" class="width">
    <h2 id="how-i-work" class="accent">How I work</h2>
    <p>
      The best work usually starts by reducing noise without flattening nuance. That means understanding what
      actually matters, where the real constraints are, and which trade-offs will still make sense later.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Efficiency:</strong> fix root causes, not symptoms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Systems thinking:</strong> connect technical and organizational dependencies so decisions age well.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ease of use:</strong> make outcomes simpler for teams and customers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Knowledge sharing:</strong> teach, document, and invite questions so teams level up together.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Delight in the details:</strong> keep experiences calm, reliable, and quietly excellent.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In practice, that means translating between business intent and implementation detail, and keeping
      delivery calm enough for useful progress to compound.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="beyond-work" class="width">
    <h2 id="beyond-work" class="accent">Beyond work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Music:</strong> electronic for decades; vinyl DJing sharpened my sense of rhythm, pacing, and flow.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Film:</strong> long-time passion and part of my career; I
        <a href="/movies?isRecommendation=true&sort=year&dir=desc">keep notes on what I watch and recommend</a
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Movement:</strong> cycling, yoga, and long walks to keep systems balanced.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Learning:</strong> cities, language, culture, food, aviation; I am drawn to how systems reflect
        the people behind them.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="contact" class="width">
    <h2 id="contact" class="accent">Say hi</h2>

    <p>When I'm not working on systems, you might find me exploring these spaces:</p>
    <ul>
      {
        allSocials.map((l) => (
          <li>
            <a href={l.url} target="_blank" rel={externalRel(l.rel)}>
              {l.name}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p>
      Head over to my <a href="/contact">contact page</a> for all the ways to reach me.
    </p>

    <p>
      <NamePronunciation pronunciation="/ʒɪl dɛsmɐʁɛ/" audioSrc="/index.flac" /><br />
      And yes, I'll still respond if your first guess is <q>Jill</q>. <span class="smiley">:-)</span>
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="micro">For timeline, roles, and technologies, see my <a href="/resume">Résumé</a>.</span>
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr />

  <section>
    <h2 class="sr-only accent" id="footnote-label">Footnotes</h2>
    <ul class="micro">
      <li>
        <a id="foot-1">[1]</a>: Staying connected became a bit of a mission too. By 2008 I even rigged my
        Nokia E51 as a UMTS modem over Bluetooth so I could stay
        <em>online</em> on the move while most people around me in Germany were still dialing in at home; <a
          href="/blog/nokia-e51-als-umts-modem-via-bluetooth-unter-ubuntu/">here's the write-up</a
        > for a little early-internet wink.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .smiley {
    color: var(--brand-accent);
    letter-spacing: -2px;
    transform: rotate(90deg);
    display: inline-block;
    margin-left: 0.2em;
    user-select: none;
  }
</style>
